  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186184172,
      "id": "5e145cb2-806a-579d-8299-8a91a6feaad1",
      "title": "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem and the Rank-Nullity Theorem"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "f378b6a2-a5a4-5ab8-a560-314e7e3e579b",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and  $\\text{Null}(A)=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2a \\\\ 3a \\\\ 3b\\end{bmatrix}\\,|\\,a,b\\in R\\right\\}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Since $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible\n\n$$\n\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{Col}(A) &=\\mathbb{R}^{3} \\\\ \\dim(\\operatorname{Col}(A)) &=3=\\operatorname{rank}(A) \\\\ \\operatorname{Null}(A) &=\\{0\\} \\\\ \\dim(\\operatorname{Null}(A)) &=0=\\operatorname{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$  \n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nThe supplied null space has the independent spanning vectors\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\;\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThus $\\text{nullity}(A)=2.$\n\nBy the rank-nullity theorem,\n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+2=3,\\qquad \\text{rank}(A)=3-2=1.\n$$\n\nHence $\\text{rank}(A)=1$ and $\\text{nullity}(A)=2$. Since rank is less than the number of columns, $A$ is not invertible and $\\det(A)=0$. Also, $\\text{Col}(A)$ has dimension 1. The number of pivot columns is 1, and the number of non-pivot columns is 2.\n\n- Statement \u201c$A$ is not invertible\u201d is true. The rank is less than the number of columns.\n\n- Statement \u201c$\\text{rank}(A)=0$\u201d is false. $\\text{rank}(A)=1$.\n\n- Statement \u201c$det(A)=0$\u201d is true. $A$ is not invertible, so $\\det(A)=0$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $A$ is not invertible; $det(A)=0$.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
